CS214 P3
--------
Authors: Angel Bomova , Astin Huynh
NetIDs: aab405 , ah1656

How to build/run
----------------
cd P3
make
./mysh
./mysh <file>
cat <file> | ./mysh

Files
-----
mysh.c - main shell loop. handles input reading, interactive vs batch mode, prompt printing, and overall control flow.
mysh.h - struct definitions, constants, and function declarations used across all files.
parser.c - tokenizes input and parses tokens into command and pipeline structures.
executor.c - executes commands, handles forking, pipelines, redirection, and process control.
builtins.c - write here
wildcard.c - handles wildcard (*) expansion for matching filenames.
utils.c - write here
Makefile - builds mysh executable.
README - README file.

Program design
--------------
Shell loop:
The shell runs in a loop:
print prompt (interactive mode only)
read input line
tokenize input
parse tokens into commands
execute commands
repeat until exit

Input handling:
Uses read() for input instead of standard I/O functions
Supports both:
interactive mode (terminal input)
batch mode (file or piped input)

Tokenization and parsing:
Input is split into tokens based on whitespace
Special tokens:
< for input redirection

> for output redirection
> | for pipelines

Parser builds a job structure:
each job contains one or more commands
each command contains:
argument list (argv)
input file (optional)
output file (optional)

Execution:
Uses fork() and execv() to run external programs
Parent process waits for child using waitpid()

Redirection:
Input redirection:
open file with open()
replace stdin using dup2()

Output redirection:
open/create file with open()
replace stdout using dup2()

Pipelines:
Multiple commands connected with pipes
pipe() used to create communication channels
each command runs in a separate process
stdout of one command connects to stdin of the next

Built-in commands:
cd:
changes current working directory using chdir()

pwd:
prints current working directory using getcwd()

which:
searches PATH to find executable location

exit:
terminates the shell

Built-ins run in the parent process unless part of a pipeline

Wildcard expansion:
Supports * wildcard for filename matching
Matches files in the current directory or specified path
If no match is found, the original token is used

Uses directory traversal functions:
opendir(), readdir(), closedir()

Signal handling:
Shell ignores SIGINT (Ctrl+C)
Child processes use default signal handling
If a child is terminated by a signal:
shell prints termination status

Batch mode behavior:
When running in batch mode:
stdin for child processes defaults to /dev/null unless redirected
prevents hanging on input

Testing
-------
Basic functionality:
pwd
cd ..
cd
which ls
which cd

External commands:
ls
ls -l
/bin/ls
notacommand

Redirection:
echo hello > out.txt
cat out.txt
cat < out.txt
pwd > file.txt
cat file.txt

Pipelines:
ls | wc
echo hello | wc
pwd | wc
which ls | wc

Wildcards:
ls *.c
ls *.h
ls nofile*.xyz
ls .*
ls subdir/*.c

Comments and syntax:

# comment

ls # comment
ls |
| ls
ls >
ls <
ls < <

Batch mode:
printf "pwd\nwhich ls\nexit\n" > test1.sh
./mysh test1.sh
cat test1.sh | ./mysh

Signal handling:
sleep 5
Ctrl+C terminates child but keeps shell running

Edge cases:
Empty input lines
Invalid commands
Nonexistent files
Invalid redirection syntax
Multiple pipes

Notes
-----
If wildcard does not match any files, it is passed unchanged

Built-in commands behave like external commands in pipelines

Shell prints exit status for non-zero exits

Program continues running after errors

Conclusion
----------
This project demonstrates process control, parsing, file descriptor manipulation, and signal handling. The shell replicates core behavior of Unix shells for the supported features and handles both interactive and batch execution correctly.
